Restrict tangent to $-\frac{\pi}{2}<x<\frac{\pi}{2}$, where it is one-to-one and takes every real value. The endpoints are excluded because tangent is undefined there.

The notation $\tan^{-1}x=\arctan x$ denotes the inverse function: its input is a tangent ratio and its output is the corresponding angle in radians. It does not mean the reciprocal $\frac{1}{\tan x}$.

Domain: all real $x$.
Range: $-\frac{\pi}{2}<y<\frac{\pi}{2}$.

The graph is increasing and odd, since $\arctan(-x)=-\arctan x$. It approaches, but never reaches, the horizontal asymptotes $y=\frac{\pi}{2}$ and $y=-\frac{\pi}{2}$.

